108→
109→\subsection{Ring Topologies and Pipelined Inference}
110→
111→The ring all-reduce algorithm~\cite{patarasuk2009bandwidth} is a well-established communication pattern for distributed computation. Prima.cpp~\cite{li2025prima} adapted a pipelined ring architecture for splitting LLMs across heterogeneous home devices, introducing pipelined-ring parallelism (PRP) to overlap disk I/O with computation and communication. However, Prima.cpp is built on llama.cpp, restricting it to GGUF-format models, and operates on static local clusters without P2P networking capabilities.
112→
113→HyperCluster extends the ring pipeline concept by: (i)~building on the Hugging Face Transformers ecosystem for broad model compatibility, (ii)~implementing the protocol over Iroh's~\cite{iroh2024} P2P networking stack with QUIC-based direct transport for internet-scale operation, and (iii)~introducing selective layer loading to dramatically reduce per-node memory requirements and model load times.
114→
115→%----------------------------------------------------------------------
116→\section{System Architecture}
117→\label{sec:system_design}
118→%----------------------------------------------------------------------
119→
120→The HyperCluster architecture comprises four interconnected subsystems: a \textbf{Network Layer} for P2P communication, a \textbf{Partitioning Layer} for dynamic model sharding, an \textbf{Execution Layer} for ring-pipeline inference, and a \textbf{Loading Layer} for memory-efficient model materialization. Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} provides an end-to-end overview.
121→
122→\begin{figure}[t]
123→\centering
124→\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{figures/architecture_iter3_0.jpg}
125→\caption{HyperCluster system architecture. Nodes discover peers via Iroh gossip, form a memory-ordered ring, selectively load only their assigned layers from safetensors, and execute pipelined inference with QUIC-based tensor forwarding.}
126→\label{fig:architecture}
127→\end{figure}
128→
129→\subsection{Network Layer: P2P Communication via Iroh and QUIC}
130→\label{sec:network_layer}
131→
132→HyperCluster builds its network layer upon \textbf{Iroh}~\cite{iroh2024}, a content-addressed networking library providing NAT traversal, gossip-based peer discovery, and document synchronization. Each node advertises its capabilities (available memory, compute class) through Iroh's document synchronization mechanism, and a \texttt{TopologyManager} maintains a real-time view of all active peers.
133→
134→\noindent\textbf{Direct Transport via QUIC.}
135→For tensor forwarding during inference, HyperCluster bypasses Iroh's document-based data exchange in favor of direct QUIC bidirectional streams (BiStreams). This provides several advantages over document-based transfer:
136→
137→\begin{itemize}
138→    \item \textbf{Low latency:} Direct stream-based RPC avoids the overhead of content-addressed blob storage and document synchronization.
139→    \item \textbf{Connection pooling:} Persistent \texttt{StreamHandle} objects maintain QUIC connections to ring neighbors, with multiplexed RPC calls over a single connection.
140→    \item \textbf{Binary framing:} Length-prefixed binary frames with msgpack serialization minimize encoding overhead compared to JSON-based protocols.
141→\end{itemize}
142→
143→The wire format for tensor transmission consists of a 4-byte metadata length prefix, followed by JSON metadata (tensor shape, dtype, position IDs, attention mask), followed by raw tensor bytes. This format supports efficient numpy array reconstruction on the receiving end.
144→
145→\noindent\textbf{Pre-warmed Connections.}
146→Before inference begins, HyperCluster establishes QUIC connections to all ring neighbors via a pre-warming phase, sending lightweight ping/pong frames. This eliminates the 6--20\,ms QUIC handshake latency that would otherwise be incurred on the first inference request.
147→
148→\subsection{Partitioning Layer: Memory-Weighted Sharding}
149→\label{sec:partitioning}
150→
151→To distribute a model with $L$ layers across $N$ heterogeneous nodes with memory capacities $M = \{m_1, m_2, \ldots, m_N\}$, HyperCluster employs a \textbf{memory-proportional partitioning strategy}. Nodes are sorted by available memory in descending order, establishing both the ring topology and the layer assignment. Each node $i$ receives a number of layers proportional to its memory fraction of the total cluster memory. The partitioning algorithm is detailed in Algorithm~\ref{alg:partitioning}.
152→
153→\begin{algorithm}[t]
154→\caption{Memory-Weighted Layer Partitioning}
155→\label{alg:partitioning}
156→\begin{algorithmic}[1]
157→\STATE \textbf{Input:} Total layers $L$, Sorted nodes $\{N_1, \ldots, N_n\}$ with memories $\{m_1, \ldots, m_n\}$
158→\STATE \textbf{Output:} Layer assignments $W = \{w_1, \ldots, w_n\}$
159→\STATE $M_{\text{total}} \leftarrow \sum_{i=1}^{n} m_i$
160→\STATE $L_{\text{current}} \leftarrow 0$
161→\FOR{$i = 1$ \TO $n$}
162→    \STATE $f_i \leftarrow m_i / M_{\text{total}}$ \COMMENT{Memory fraction}
163→    \IF{$i = n$}
164→        \STATE $\ell_i \leftarrow L - L_{\text{current}}$ \COMMENT{Last node gets remaining}
165→    \ELSE
166→        \STATE $\ell_i \leftarrow \max(1, \lfloor f_i \times L \rfloor)$ \COMMENT{At least 1 layer}
167→    \ENDIF
168→    \STATE $w_i \leftarrow \texttt{LayerWindow}(L_{\text{current}},\; L_{\text{current}} + \ell_i - 1,\; L)$
169→    \STATE $L_{\text{current}} \leftarrow L_{\text{current}} + \ell_i$
170→\ENDFOR
171→\RETURN $W$
172→\end{algorithmic}
173→\end{algorithm}
174→
175→For modern transformer architectures, layers have approximately uniform memory requirements, making layer count a reliable proxy for resource allocation. The resulting \texttt{LayerWindow} objects define contiguous layer ranges per node, which are used by both the loading layer (Section~\ref{sec:selective_loading}) and the execution layer (Section~\ref{sec:ring_pipeline}).
176→
177→\subsection{Loading Layer: Selective Layer Loading from Safetensors}
178→\label{sec:selective_loading}
179→
180→Traditional model loading via \texttt{from\_pretrained()} loads \textit{all} model weights into memory and then frees unneeded layers---a process that is both slow and memory-wasteful when a node requires only a subset of layers. HyperCluster introduces \textbf{selective layer loading}, which materializes only the tensors required by each node's assigned shard.
181→
182→\noindent\textbf{Loading Procedure.} The selective loading process consists of five steps:
183→
184→\begin{enumerate}
185→    \item \textbf{Empty Skeleton Creation:} Using \texttt{init\_empty\_weights()}, create the full model architecture on a \texttt{meta} device. This allocates zero bytes, producing only the computational graph.
186→
187→    \item \textbf{Weight Key Filtering:} Determine which tensor keys are needed based on the assigned layer range. For a node assigned layers $[s, e]$ out of $L$ total:
188→    \begin{itemize}
189→        \item If $s = 0$ (first shard): include embedding weights (\texttt{embed\_tokens}).
190→        \item If $e = L-1$ (last shard): include final layer norm and \texttt{lm\_head} weights.
191→        \item Always: include weights for layers $s$ through $e$.
192→    \end{itemize}
193→
194→    \item \textbf{File-to-Key Mapping:} Parse the safetensors index file to build a mapping from checkpoint files to the subset of needed keys in each file, minimizing I/O by opening only files containing relevant weights.
195→
196→    \item \textbf{Targeted Materialization:} For each needed key, use \texttt{safe\_open()} to read the tensor directly from the safetensors file and \texttt{set\_module\_tensor\_to\_device()} to place it on the target device. Unneeded layers remain on the \texttt{meta} device at zero cost.
197→
198→    \item \textbf{Weight-Tying Restoration:} For models with \texttt{tie\_word\_embeddings=True} (Section~\ref{sec:weight_tying}), explicitly re-tie the language model head to the embedding weights.
199→\end{enumerate}
200→
201→\noindent\textbf{Memory Efficiency.} Table~\ref{tab:selective_loading} summarizes the loading performance on Qwen3-0.6B.
202→
203→\begin{table}[t]
204→\centering
205→\caption{Selective layer loading performance on Qwen3-0.6B (28 layers). Selective loading achieves $4\times$ faster load time and $80\%$ lower peak memory.}
206→\label{tab:selective_loading}
207→\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
208→\toprule
209→\textbf{Configuration} & \textbf{Layers} & \textbf{Load Time (s)} & \textbf{Peak Memory (MB)} & \textbf{Speedup} \\
210→\midrule
211→Full load (\texttt{from\_pretrained}) & 0--27 & 1.52 & 7.0 & 1.0$\times$ \\
212→HEAD shard (selective) & 0--17 & 0.36 & 1.3 & 4.2$\times$ \\
213→WORKER shard (selective) & 18--27 & 0.32 & 1.3 & 4.8$\times$ \\
214→\bottomrule
215→\end{tabular}
216→\end{table}
217→
218→\subsection{Weight-Tying Preservation}
219→\label{sec:weight_tying}
220→
221→Many modern LLMs (e.g., Llama-3.2~\cite{grattafiori2024llama}, Qwen3~\cite{yang2025qwen3}) use \textit{weight tying}, where the language model head (\texttt{lm\_head}) shares weights with the token embedding layer (\texttt{embed\_tokens}). In standard \texttt{from\_pretrained()} loading, this sharing is implicit: the framework recognizes the tie and avoids storing duplicate weights. However, selective loading via \texttt{init\_empty\_weights()} followed by per-tensor \texttt{set\_module\_tensor\_to\_device()} breaks this implicit tie, leaving \texttt{lm\_head} on the meta device with uninitialized (all-zero) weights. This produces all-zero logits, leading to random token sampling and gibberish output.
222→
223→HyperCluster addresses this with explicit weight-tying restoration after selective loading:
224→
225→\begin{itemize}
226→    \item \textbf{First-and-last shard} (single node holding both embeddings and lm\_head): After loading, explicitly set \texttt{lm\_head.weight = embed\_tokens.weight}, re-establishing the tie.
227→    \item \textbf{Last-only shard} (node without embeddings but with lm\_head): Load \texttt{embed\_tokens.weight} from the safetensors file and assign it to \texttt{lm\_head.weight}, even though the node does not use the embedding layer for forward computation.
228→    \item \textbf{Verification:} After restoration, verify that \texttt{lm\_head.weight} resides on an actual device (not meta) and contains non-zero values.
229→\end{itemize}
230→
231→\subsection{Execution Layer: Ring-Pipeline Inference}
232→\label{sec:ring_pipeline}
233→
234→Following layer assignment and selective loading, nodes form a deterministic logical ring $N_0 \rightarrow N_1 \rightarrow \cdots \rightarrow N_{n-1} \rightarrow N_0$, where rank corresponds to the memory-based ordering. Node $N_0$ (rank~0) holds the initial layers including token embeddings and serves as the entry point for inference requests.
235→
236→\noindent\textbf{Ring Position and Inference State.}
237→Each node maintains a \texttt{RingPosition} recording its rank, world size, and the identities of its predecessor and successor in the ring. The inference state flowing through the ring consists of:
238→
239→\begin{itemize}
240→    \item \textbf{Hidden states:} The intermediate activations ($\mathbb{R}^{1 \times d}$ for single-token generation), passed from node to node.
241→    \item \textbf{Position IDs:} Required for Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE), incremented at each generation step.
242→    \item \textbf{Attention mask:} Grows with each generated token to maintain causal attention.
243→    \item \textbf{KV cache:} \textit{Not transmitted}---each node maintains a local cache only for its assigned layers, ensuring that only the compact hidden states flow through the ring.
244→\end{itemize}
245→
246→\noindent\textbf{Pipeline Operation.}
247→Inference proceeds in two phases:
248→
249→\begin{enumerate}
250→    \item \textbf{Prefill Phase:} The initial prompt tokens are processed in a single forward pass through the ring. Each node executes its assigned layers sequentially, passing hidden states to its successor via the QUIC direct transport.
251→
252→    \item \textbf{Autoregressive Generation Phase:} For each new token, the single-token input traverses the entire ring. The final node ($N_{n-1}$) computes output logits, samples the next token using nucleus sampling with configurable temperature and top-$p$ parameters, and the token is propagated back to the head node to begin the next generation step.
253→\end{enumerate}
254→
255→\noindent\textbf{Cycle Calculation.}
256→If a model has more layers than can be covered in a single pass through the ring (i.e., the sum of all layer windows is less than the total layer count), multiple cycles through the ring are required. The number of cycles is computed as $k = \lceil L / \sum_{i} w_i \rceil$, where $w_i$ is the layer count assigned to node $i$. In practice, with sufficient nodes, $k=1$.
257→
258→The complete autoregressive generation loop for a 3-node cluster is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:ring_pipeline}.
259→
260→\begin{figure}[t]
261→\centering
262→\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{figures/ring_pipeline_iter3_0.jpg}
263→\caption{Ring pipeline autoregressive generation across 3 nodes (28 layers). \textbf{Top:} Prefill phase---prompt tokens traverse the ring once, with each node processing its assigned layers and forwarding hidden states via QUIC. \textbf{Bottom:} Generation phase---each new token cycles through all nodes; the final node samples the next token and feeds it back to the head node.}
264→\label{fig:ring_pipeline}
265→\end{figure}
266→
267→\subsection{Transformer Layer Sharding via Dynamic Wrapping}
268→\label{sec:sharding}
269→
270→The sharding engine implements runtime layer extraction through the \texttt{TransformersShard} wrapper class, which dynamically isolates assigned layers from any Hugging Face \texttt{AutoModelForCausalLM} instance. The wrapper categorizes shards by their role:
271→
272→\begin{itemize}
273→    \item \textbf{Initial Shard ($N_0$):} Executes token embedding (\texttt{embed\_tokens}) followed by layers $[0, k_0]$.
274→    \item \textbf{Intermediate Shards ($N_i$, $0 < i < n-1$):} Process layers $[k_{i-1}+1, k_i]$ on received hidden states.
275→    \item \textbf{Final Shard ($N_{n-1}$):} Executes layers $[k_{n-2}+1, L-1]$, applies final layer normalization, and projects to vocabulary via the language model head.
276→\end{itemize}
277→
278→\noindent\textbf{Critical Implementation Details.}
279→Two implementation details are essential for correct distributed inference:
280→
281→\begin{enumerate}
282→    \item \textbf{Config Patching:} The model's \texttt{num\_hidden\_layers} configuration is overridden to match the shard's layer count. This is critical because the \texttt{DynamicCache} (used in transformers $\geq$ 5.2.0) pre-allocates cache storage based on this value; without patching, cache indices are misaligned, producing incorrect attention computations.
283→
284→    \item \textbf{Layer Re-indexing:} The \texttt{layer\_idx} attribute on each attention module is re-indexed to $[0, |\text{shard}|-1]$, ensuring that KV cache entries are stored at the correct indices within the node-local cache, regardless of the layer's position in the full model.
285→\end{enumerate}
286→
287→\noindent\textbf{Distributed State Management.}
288→For correct autoregressive generation, each forward pass transmits hidden states along with position IDs and the attention mask. The KV cache is \textit{not} transmitted between nodes; instead, each node maintains and updates a local cache only for its assigned layers. This design ensures that only the hidden states ($\mathbb{R}^{1 \times d}$, typically a few kilobytes) flow through the ring, while the much larger KV cache (scaling with sequence length and layer count) remains node-local.
289→
290→%----------------------------------------------------------------------
291→\section{Evaluation and Results}
292→\label{sec:evaluation}
293→%----------------------------------------------------------------------
294→
295→\subsection{Experimental Setup}
296→
297→\noindent\textbf{Hardware Configuration.}
298→Our testbed consists of three heterogeneous consumer devices, summarized in Table~\ref{tab:devices}.
299→
300→\begin{table}[t]
301→\centering
302→\caption{Testbed device configuration. Nodes communicate over the Iroh P2P network across separate physical networks.}
303→\label{tab:devices}
304→\begin{tabular}{lccc}
305→\toprule
306→ & \textbf{Device 1 (D1)} & \textbf{Device 2 (D2)} & \textbf{Device 3 (D3)} \\
307→\midrule
308→Device & MacBook Air M2 & Mac Mini & Linux Laptop \\
309→CPU & Apple M2 & Apple Silicon & Intel i5 \\
310→RAM & 16 GB & 8 GB & 8 GB \\
311→GPU & Apple Silicon & Apple Silicon & None (CPU-only) \\
312→Role & Compute Provider & Compute Provider & Compute Provider \\
313→\bottomrule
314→\end{tabular}
315→\end{table}
316→
317→\noindent\textbf{Models.}
318→We evaluate on three models: Qwen3-0.6B~\cite{yang2025qwen3} (28 layers, $d=1024$), Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct~\cite{grattafiori2024llama} (16 layers, $d=2048$), and Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct~\cite{grattafiori2024llama} (28 layers, $d=3072$). All three use \texttt{tie\_word\_embeddings=True}, exercising our weight-tying preservation mechanism.
319→
320→\noindent\textbf{Metrics.}
321→We report three primary metrics: (1)~\textbf{Time to First Token (TTFT)}: latency from query submission to the first generated token; (2)~\textbf{Average Time Per Token (ATPT)}: mean latency per generated token during autoregressive generation; and (3)~\textbf{Tokens per second}: throughput ($1000 / \text{ATPT}$). All metrics are averaged across 32, 64, and 128 token generation sequences.
322→
323→\subsection{Inference Performance}
324→
325→Table~\ref{tab:latency} presents the latency breakdown across 1-, 2-, and 3-node configurations.
326→
327→\begin{table}[t]
328→\centering
329→\caption{Latency breakdown across cluster configurations. Moving from 1 to multiple nodes introduces communication overhead dominated by QUIC tensor transfers over the P2P network.}
330→\label{tab:latency}
331→\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
332→\toprule
333→\textbf{Setup} & \textbf{TTFT (ms)} & \textbf{ATPT (ms)} & \textbf{Tokens/sec} \\
334→\midrule
335→\textbf{1-Node} & & & \\
336→\quad Qwen3 0.6B & 600.46 & 78.70 & 11.18 \\
337→\quad Llama 3.2 1B & 942.04 & 130.31 & 6.96 \\
338→\quad Llama 3.2 3B & 1,847.32 & 292.15 & 3.42 \\
339→\midrule
