352→
353→\noindent\textbf{Single-Node Baseline.}
354→On a single node (MacBook Air M2), Qwen3-0.6B achieves 11.18 tokens/sec with 600\,ms TTFT, Llama-3.2-1B reaches 6.96 tokens/sec with 942\,ms TTFT, and the larger Llama-3.2-3B produces 3.42 tokens/sec with 1,847\,ms TTFT. These baselines establish the performance ceiling without communication overhead and confirm that per-token compute cost scales with model size.
355→
356→\noindent\textbf{Multi-Node Scaling.}
357→Moving to 2 and 3 nodes introduces communication overhead dominated by QUIC tensor transfers over the P2P network. For Llama-3.2-1B, TTFT increases from 942\,ms to 3.0\,s (2-node) and 2.1\,s (3-node), while ATPT increases from 130\,ms to 314\,ms (2-node) and 303\,ms (3-node). Notably, the 3-node configuration achieves \textit{lower} per-token latency than 2-node for Llama-3.2-1B (2.91 vs.\ 1.68 tokens/sec), because distributing fewer layers per node reduces per-hop compute time sufficiently to offset the additional network hop.
358→
359→The larger Llama-3.2-3B demonstrates that HyperCluster can distribute models up to 3B parameters across consumer devices, achieving 1.72 tokens/sec on 2 nodes and 1.15 tokens/sec on 3 nodes. Its larger hidden dimension ($d=3072$) increases tensor transfer volume per hop, making communication overhead more pronounced.
360→
