% Checkmark and cross shortcuts
\newcommand{\cmark}{\ding{51}}
\newcommand{\xmark}{\ding{55}}

% Configuration for code listings
\lstset{%
	basicstyle={\footnotesize\ttfamily},
	numbers=left,numberstyle=\footnotesize,xleftmargin=2em,
	aboveskip=10pt,belowskip=10pt,
	showstringspaces=false,tabsize=2,breaklines=true}

\begin{document}
%
\title{HyperCluster: Decentralized Large Language Model Inference over Peer-to-Peer Wireless Networks}
%
\titlerunning{HyperCluster: Decentralized LLM Inference}
%
\author{Anonymous Submission}
%
\authorrunning{Anonymous et al.}
%
\institute{Affiliations Placeholder}
%
\maketitle
%
\begin{abstract}
The substantial memory and computational requirements of large language models (LLMs) hinder their deployment on individual resource-constrained devices. This paper introduces HyperCluster, a framework for fully decentralized collaborative inference over peer-to-peer wireless networks. HyperCluster presents four core innovations: (1)~a ring-based pipelined inference protocol where nodes deterministically self-organize into a computational ring based on device capabilities, passing intermediate states directly between peers via QUIC-based direct transport; (2)~a generalizable model sharding methodology built upon the Hugging Face Transformers library that automatically partitions any dense LLM across heterogeneous devices according to available compute and memory; (3)~selective layer loading from safetensors files, which materializes only the tensor weights required by each node's assigned shard, reducing load time by up to $4\times$ and peak memory by $80\%$ compared to full model loading; and (4)~a robust weight-tying mechanism that preserves correctness for models with shared embedding and language model head weights. We validate HyperCluster on a heterogeneous cluster of consumer-grade devices, demonstrating distributed inference of models up to 3~billion parameters with comprehensive latency and throughput analysis across 1-, 2-, and 3-node configurations.

\keywords{Distributed AI \and Peer-to-Peer Networks \and LLM Inference \and Model Sharding \and Ring Pipeline \and Selective Layer Loading}
\end{abstract}

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}
%----------------------------------------------------------------------

The proliferation of large language models (LLMs) has created an unprecedented demand for computational resources, with state-of-the-art models requiring tens to hundreds of gigabytes of memory that far exceed the capacity of typical consumer and edge devices. While cloud-based services provide access to these models, they introduce privacy concerns, increase reliance on centralized infrastructure, and incur costs that scale linearly with usage---concerns that are particularly acute in wireless and edge computing scenarios~\cite{li2025prima}.

An alternative paradigm is \textit{collaborative inference}, where multiple devices in a peer-to-peer (P2P) network pool their resources to collectively execute a model that is too large for any single device. However, realizing this vision in a decentralized wireless environment presents significant challenges. Frameworks designed for distributed training in data centers, such as Megatron-LM~\cite{shoeybi2019megatron} and DeepSpeed~\cite{rasley2020deepspeed}, depend on high-bandwidth, low-latency interconnects and stable cluster memberships---assumptions that do not hold in dynamic P2P networks. Existing distributed inference systems either require centralized coordination~\cite{borzunov2023petals}, are restricted to local networks~\cite{exo}, or lack broad model compatibility.

This paper introduces HyperCluster, a framework for fully decentralized collaborative inference designed specifically for heterogeneous devices over P2P networks across the internet. We make the following contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Decentralized Ring-Pipeline Protocol:} A coordinator-free inference protocol where nodes self-organize into a deterministic computational ring based on their advertised capabilities, with QUIC-based direct transport for low-latency tensor forwarding and pre-warmed connections to eliminate setup overhead.

    \item \textbf{Generalizable Transformers-Based Sharding Engine:} A dynamic model sharding methodology that integrates directly with the Hugging Face Transformers library~\cite{wolf-etal-2020-transformers}, automatically partitioning dense, off-the-shelf LLMs across heterogeneous devices based on available memory without requiring model-specific modifications.

    \item \textbf{Selective Layer Loading:} A memory-efficient model loading strategy that creates an empty model skeleton on a meta device and materializes only the tensors required by each node's assigned shard directly from safetensors files, achieving up to $4\times$ faster load times and $80\%$ lower peak memory compared to full model loading.

    \item \textbf{Weight-Tying Preservation:} A mechanism to correctly handle models with tied embedding weights (e.g., Llama-3.2, Qwen3), which do not store duplicate weights in checkpoint files, ensuring correct inference output under selective loading.
\end{enumerate}

We validate HyperCluster on a heterogeneous cluster of consumer-grade devices, providing detailed latency breakdown, throughput analysis, and memory efficiency measurements across multiple model families and cluster configurations.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Background and Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}
%----------------------------------------------------------------------

This research builds upon two primary domains: (1)~large-scale parallel systems designed for centralized data centers, and (2)~emerging frameworks for decentralized, peer-to-peer computation.

\subsection{Model Parallelism in Centralized Environments}

The challenge of partitioning large neural models has been thoroughly investigated within data center environments. Megatron-LM~\cite{shoeybi2019megatron} and DeepSpeed~\cite{rasley2020deepspeed} introduced foundational techniques for distributing models across multiple GPUs, while Alpa~\cite{zheng2022alpa} refined these with automated inter- and intra-operator parallelism. However, these systems assume stable hardware, high-bandwidth interconnects (e.g., NVLink, InfiniBand), and homogeneous compute resources---assumptions that are incompatible with dynamic, bandwidth-constrained P2P networks.

\subsection{Decentralized and Peer-to-Peer Inference}

Recent work has begun to explore distributed inference over less reliable networks. Petals~\cite{borzunov2023petals} demonstrated collaborative inference where users contribute GPU resources, but relies on central authority for peer discovery and assumes stable, high-bandwidth connections. Exo~\cite{exo} enables distributed inference by sharding models across a ``home cluster'' of consumer devices with memory-weighted partitioning, but is restricted to local networks, does not address NAT traversal, and supports only a limited set of model families. Lattica~\cite{yang2025lattica} provides cross-NAT communication for decentralized AI but operates as a networking layer without application-level inference protocols.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\caption{Comparison of distributed on-device LLM inference systems. HyperCluster uniquely combines internet-scale P2P networking, selective loading, and broad model support. (Abbr.: Sel.\ Load = Selective Layer Loading, Wt.\ Tie = Weight-Tying Support)}
\label{tab:comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{System} & \textbf{Type} & \textbf{P2P} & \textbf{NAT} & \textbf{Sel.\ Load} & \textbf{Wt.\ Tie} & \textbf{Model Scope} & \textbf{Backend} \\
\midrule
Petals~\cite{borzunov2023petals} & PP & \xmark & \xmark & \xmark & \xmark & Limited & GPU \\
Exo~\cite{exo} & PP & \cmark & \xmark & \xmark & \xmark & Limited & CPU/GPU \\
Prima.cpp~\cite{li2025prima} & PRP & \xmark & \xmark & \xmark & N/A & llama.cpp & CPU\&GPU \\
dllama & TP & \xmark & \xmark & \xmark & \xmark & Limited & CPU \\
\midrule
\textbf{HyperCluster} & \textbf{Ring-PP} & \cmark & \cmark & \cmark & \cmark & \textbf{Any HF} & \textbf{CPU/GPU} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Ring Topologies and Pipelined Inference}

The ring all-reduce algorithm~\cite{patarasuk2009bandwidth} is a well-established communication pattern for distributed computation. Prima.cpp~\cite{li2025prima} adapted a pipelined ring architecture for splitting LLMs across heterogeneous home devices, introducing pipelined-ring parallelism (PRP) to overlap disk I/O with computation and communication. However, Prima.cpp is built on llama.cpp, restricting it to GGUF-format models, and operates on static local clusters without P2P networking capabilities.

HyperCluster extends the ring pipeline concept by: (i)~building on the Hugging Face Transformers ecosystem for broad model compatibility, (ii)~implementing the protocol over Iroh's~\cite{iroh2024} P2P networking stack with QUIC-based direct transport for internet-scale operation, and (iii)~introducing selective layer loading to dramatically reduce per-node memory requirements and model load times.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{System Architecture}
\label{sec:system_design}
%----------------------------------------------------------------------

The HyperCluster architecture comprises four interconnected subsystems: a \textbf{Network Layer} for P2P communication, a \textbf{Partitioning Layer} for dynamic model sharding, an \textbf{Execution Layer} for ring-pipeline inference, and a \textbf{Loading Layer} for memory-efficient model materialization. Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} provides an end-to-end overview.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{figures/architecture_iter3_0.jpg}
\caption{HyperCluster system architecture. Nodes discover peers via Iroh gossip, form a memory-ordered ring, selectively load only their assigned layers from safetensors, and execute pipelined inference with QUIC-based tensor forwarding.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Network Layer: P2P Communication via Iroh and QUIC}
\label{sec:network_layer}

HyperCluster builds its network layer upon \textbf{Iroh}~\cite{iroh2024}, a content-addressed networking library providing NAT traversal, gossip-based peer discovery, and document synchronization. Each node advertises its capabilities (available memory, compute class) through Iroh's document synchronization mechanism, and a \texttt{TopologyManager} maintains a real-time view of all active peers.

\noindent\textbf{Direct Transport via QUIC.}
For tensor forwarding during inference, HyperCluster bypasses Iroh's document-based data exchange in favor of direct QUIC bidirectional streams (BiStreams). This provides several advantages over document-based transfer:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Low latency:} Direct stream-based RPC avoids the overhead of content-addressed blob storage and document synchronization.
    \item \textbf{Connection pooling:} Persistent \texttt{StreamHandle} objects maintain QUIC connections to ring neighbors, with multiplexed RPC calls over a single connection.
    \item \textbf{Binary framing:} Length-prefixed binary frames with msgpack serialization minimize encoding overhead compared to JSON-based protocols.
\end{itemize}

The wire format for tensor transmission consists of a 4-byte metadata length prefix, followed by JSON metadata (tensor shape, dtype, position IDs, attention mask), followed by raw tensor bytes. This format supports efficient numpy array reconstruction on the receiving end.

\noindent\textbf{Pre-warmed Connections.}
Before inference begins, HyperCluster establishes QUIC connections to all ring neighbors via a pre-warming phase, sending lightweight ping/pong frames. This eliminates the 6--20\,ms QUIC handshake latency that would otherwise be incurred on the first inference request.

\subsection{Partitioning Layer: Memory-Weighted Sharding}
\label{sec:partitioning}

To distribute a model with $L$ layers across $N$ heterogeneous nodes with memory capacities $M = \{m_1, m_2, \ldots, m_N\}$, HyperCluster employs a \textbf{memory-proportional partitioning strategy}. Nodes are sorted by available memory in descending order, establishing both the ring topology and the layer assignment. Each node $i$ receives a number of layers proportional to its memory fraction of the total cluster memory. The partitioning algorithm is detailed in Algorithm~\ref{alg:partitioning}.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Memory-Weighted Layer Partitioning}
\label{alg:partitioning}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE \textbf{Input:} Total layers $L$, Sorted nodes $\{N_1, \ldots, N_n\}$ with memories $\{m_1, \ldots, m_n\}$
\STATE \textbf{Output:} Layer assignments $W = \{w_1, \ldots, w_n\}$
\STATE $M_{\text{total}} \leftarrow \sum_{i=1}^{n} m_i$
\STATE $L_{\text{current}} \leftarrow 0$
\FOR{$i = 1$ \TO $n$}
    \STATE $f_i \leftarrow m_i / M_{\text{total}}$ \COMMENT{Memory fraction}
    \IF{$i = n$}
        \STATE $\ell_i \leftarrow L - L_{\text{current}}$ \COMMENT{Last node gets remaining}
    \ELSE
        \STATE $\ell_i \leftarrow \max(1, \lfloor f_i \times L \rfloor)$ \COMMENT{At least 1 layer}
    \ENDIF
    \STATE $w_i \leftarrow \texttt{LayerWindow}(L_{\text{current}},\; L_{\text{current}} + \ell_i - 1,\; L)$
    \STATE $L_{\text{current}} \leftarrow L_{\text{current}} + \ell_i$
\ENDFOR
\RETURN $W$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

For modern transformer architectures, layers have approximately uniform memory requirements, making layer count a reliable proxy for resource allocation. The resulting \texttt{LayerWindow} objects define contiguous layer ranges per node, which are used by both the loading layer (Section~\ref{sec:selective_loading}) and the execution layer (Section~\ref{sec:ring_pipeline}).

\subsection{Loading Layer: Selective Layer Loading from Safetensors}
\label{sec:selective_loading}

Traditional model loading via \texttt{from\_pretrained()} loads \textit{all} model weights into memory and then frees unneeded layers---a process that is both slow and memory-wasteful when a node requires only a subset of layers. HyperCluster introduces \textbf{selective layer loading}, which materializes only the tensors required by each node's assigned shard.

\noindent\textbf{Loading Procedure.} The selective loading process consists of five steps:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Empty Skeleton Creation:} Using \texttt{init\_empty\_weights()}, create the full model architecture on a \texttt{meta} device. This allocates zero bytes, producing only the computational graph.

    \item \textbf{Weight Key Filtering:} Determine which tensor keys are needed based on the assigned layer range. For a node assigned layers $[s, e]$ out of $L$ total:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item If $s = 0$ (first shard): include embedding weights (\texttt{embed\_tokens}).
        \item If $e = L-1$ (last shard): include final layer norm and \texttt{lm\_head} weights.
        \item Always: include weights for layers $s$ through $e$.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{File-to-Key Mapping:} Parse the safetensors index file to build a mapping from checkpoint files to the subset of needed keys in each file, minimizing I/O by opening only files containing relevant weights.

    \item \textbf{Targeted Materialization:} For each needed key, use \texttt{safe\_open()} to read the tensor directly from the safetensors file and \texttt{set\_module\_tensor\_to\_device()} to place it on the target device. Unneeded layers remain on the \texttt{meta} device at zero cost.

    \item \textbf{Weight-Tying Restoration:} For models with \texttt{tie\_word\_embeddings=True} (Section~\ref{sec:weight_tying}), explicitly re-tie the language model head to the embedding weights.
\end{enumerate}

