\subsection{Weight-Tying Preservation}
\label{sec:weight_tying}

Many modern LLMs (e.g., Llama-3.2~\cite{grattafiori2024llama}, Qwen3~\cite{yang2025qwen3}) use \textit{weight tying}, where the language model head (\texttt{lm\_head}) shares weights with the token embedding layer (\texttt{embed\_tokens}). In standard \texttt{from\_pretrained()} loading, this sharing is implicit: the framework recognizes the tie and avoids storing duplicate weights. However, selective loading via \texttt{init\_empty\_weights()} followed by per-tensor \texttt{set\_module\_tensor\_to\_device()} breaks this implicit tie, leaving \texttt{lm\_head} on the meta device with uninitialized (all-zero) weights. This produces all-zero logits, leading to random token sampling and gibberish output.

HyperCluster addresses this with explicit weight-tying restoration after selective loading:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{First-and-last shard} (single node holding both embeddings and lm\_head): After loading, explicitly set \texttt{lm\_head.weight = embed\_tokens.weight}, re-establishing the tie.
    \item \textbf{Last-only shard} (node without embeddings but with lm\_head): Load \texttt{embed\_tokens.weight} from the safetensors file and assign it to \texttt{lm\_head.weight}, even though the node does not use the embedding layer for forward computation.
    \item \textbf{Verification:} After restoration, verify that \texttt{lm\_head.weight} resides on an actual device (not meta) and contains non-zero values.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Execution Layer: Ring-Pipeline Inference}
\label{sec:ring_pipeline}

Following layer assignment and selective loading, nodes form a deterministic logical ring $N_0 \rightarrow N_1 \rightarrow \cdots \rightarrow N_{n-1} \rightarrow N_0$, where rank corresponds to the memory-based ordering. Node $N_0$ (rank~0) holds the initial layers including token embeddings and serves as the entry point for inference requests.

\noindent\textbf{Ring Position and Inference State.}
Each node maintains a \texttt{RingPosition} recording its rank, world size, and the identities of its predecessor and successor in the ring. The inference state flowing through the ring consists of:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Hidden states:} The intermediate activations ($\mathbb{R}^{1 \times d}$ for single-token generation), passed from node to node.
    \item \textbf{Position IDs:} Required for Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE), incremented at each generation step.
    \item \textbf{Attention mask:} Grows with each generated token to maintain causal attention.
    \item \textbf{KV cache:} \textit{Not transmitted}---each node maintains a local cache only for its assigned layers, ensuring that only the compact hidden states flow through the ring.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Pipeline Operation.}
Inference proceeds in two phases:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Prefill Phase:} The initial prompt tokens are processed in a single forward pass through the ring. Each node executes its assigned layers sequentially, passing hidden states to its successor via the QUIC direct transport.

    \item \textbf{Autoregressive Generation Phase:} For each new token, the single-token input traverses the entire ring. The final node ($N_{n-1}$) computes output logits, samples the next token using nucleus sampling with configurable temperature and top-$p$ parameters, and the token is propagated back to the head node to begin the next generation step.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{Cycle Calculation.}
If a model has more layers than can be covered in a single pass through the ring (i.e., the sum of all layer windows is less than the total layer count), multiple cycles through the ring are required. The number of cycles is computed as $k = \lceil L / \sum_{i} w_i \rceil$, where $w_i$ is the layer count assigned to node $i$. In practice, with sufficient nodes, $k=1$.

The complete autoregressive generation loop for a 3-node cluster is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:ring_pipeline}.

