\end{figure}

\subsection{Transformer Layer Sharding via Dynamic Wrapping}
\label{sec:sharding}

The sharding engine implements runtime layer extraction through the \texttt{TransformersShard} wrapper class, which dynamically isolates assigned layers from any Hugging Face \texttt{AutoModelForCausalLM} instance. The wrapper categorizes shards by their role:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Initial Shard ($N_0$):} Executes token embedding (\texttt{embed\_tokens}) followed by layers $[0, k_0]$.
    \item \textbf{Intermediate Shards ($N_i$, $0 < i < n-1$):} Process layers $[k_{i-1}+1, k_i]$ on received hidden states.
    \item \textbf{Final Shard ($N_{n-1}$):} Executes layers $[k_{n-2}+1, L-1]$, applies final layer normalization, and projects to vocabulary via the language model head.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Critical Implementation Details.}
Two implementation details are essential for correct distributed inference:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Config Patching:} The model's \texttt{num\_hidden\_layers} configuration is overridden to match the shard's layer count. This is critical because the \texttt{DynamicCache} (used in transformers $\geq$ 5.2.0) pre-allocates cache storage based on this value; without patching, cache indices are misaligned, producing incorrect attention computations.

    \item \textbf{Layer Re-indexing:} The \texttt{layer\_idx} attribute on each attention module is re-indexed to $[0, |\text{shard}|-1]$, ensuring that KV cache entries are stored at the correct indices within the node-local cache, regardless of the layer's position in the full model.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{Distributed State Management.}
For correct autoregressive generation, each forward pass transmits hidden states along with position IDs and the attention mask. The KV cache is \textit{not} transmitted between nodes; instead, each node maintains and updates a local cache only for its assigned layers. This design ensures that only the hidden states ($\mathbb{R}^{1 \times d}$, typically a few kilobytes) flow through the ring, while the much larger KV cache (scaling with sequence length and layer count) remains node-local.

